WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.520
Good morning, John.
Thank you for your lovely Esther Day video.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:07.200
Today I want to talk to you about a really weird reason why it is worse for conservation

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.440
to have a Republican in the White House than to have somebody from the Democratic party.

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:13.700
You might think, "Hey that's obvious Republicans hate the environment."

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:17.180
Ah, not really.
Uh, it's... more complicated than that it turns out.

00:00:17.180 --> 00:00:19.520
Let's be honest, both parties like resource extraction.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:23.460
It keeps energy prices low and it makes us
less dependent on other countries.

00:00:23.500 --> 00:00:26.560
President Obama did a great job of keeping
gas and energy prices low

00:00:26.560 --> 00:00:28.920
by having a whole lot of fracking happen in America

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:32.720
And there are a lot of reasons why conservative people in the US are totally into land conservation.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.600
Specifically, they like to use that for hunting and fishing and recreation of various kinds

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:41.040
But unless your dad the Florida state director
of the nature conservancy, which our dad was,

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:47.420
you probably don't know about this law
that's very obscure and really amazing and popular.

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:50.880
It's called the Pittman-Robertson Act. 
Named after the two senators who thought it up.

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:53.339
It was signed into law in 1937 by FDR.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:58.300
And the idea was one of the things that makes America really interesting is that we have a lot of open space

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.640
in a way that of the places where a lot of Americans came from -- Europe -- did not.

00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:06.239
But America is growing fast, land values are going up, wildlife management is all over the place,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:10.820
so the idea is we're going to take existing revenue
from a tax on guns and bullets

00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:14.140
and put that toward
managing wildlife and conserving land.

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.420
And in 1937, the main reason people bought
guns and ammo was to hunt,

00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:21.280
so basically you're just taxing hunters, 
specifically by taxing guns,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:24.740
and saying we're going to use this
to do stuff that you want us to do:

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:28.760
to protect the land so that there's still places
for people to recreate and shoot stuff

00:01:28.760 --> 00:01:32.960
-- and also so that hunting won't become what it mostly was in England, which was a rich person sport --

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:34.740
to keep it accessible to more people.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:38.159
Nowadays, guns and ammo are bought for
a wider variety of reasons.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:42.140
Hunting still, for sure, but interestingly one of
the biggest reasons people buy guns and ammo

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:48.380
is out of fear that they will soon not be able to buy certain types of guns or guns and ammunition at all.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:52.440
And as any good marketing person will tell you, the idea that something is available for a limited time only

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.080
is really great at motivating people to buy stuff now.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.980
Which is why, during Obama's eight years in office, Pittman-Robertson revenue went from

00:01:58.980 --> 00:02:03.990
310 million dollars to a peak of over 800 million dollars.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:09.877
And suddenly states had three times more money
for their conservation and wildlife biology programs

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.263
than they used to have
and they even had a hard time spending all of it!

00:02:13.263 --> 00:02:16.760
A lot of it went to local and national land trusts
to acquire new land

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:22.100
and of course that land has to be, uh, like,
open to hunters but it's also not open to development.

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.100
And interestingly, as the Pittman-Robertson revenue has increased,

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:29.760
the number of licensed hunters is going down. It's just not as popular a hobby as it once was.

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.489
So Pittman-Robertson is protecting wildlife that's getting hunted less and less.

00:02:33.489 --> 00:02:35.600
And nobody doesn't like that, though.

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:39.560
People who buy guns, even if they don't hunt,
are proud and happy to support hunters,

00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.240
and people who might be in favor of
more gun regulations in America, like me,

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:47.440
also are probably more or less happy
that more land is getting conserved.

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:51.840
To be clear, if you want to support conservation,
the best thing to do is to donate to a local land trust

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:53.639
rather than buy a bunch of bullets

00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.200
But I find it fascinating that a surge
in the purchase of firearms and ammunition

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:02.460
inspired by a fear that those things
are going to get taken away from you

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:04.800
by the person who is in the White House at that moment

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:08.640
actually did amazing things for
wildlife conservation over the last eight years.

00:03:09.060 --> 00:03:10.220
It's just weird.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:11.960
John, you recently sent me a text. It said:

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:16.949
I forgot until just now that you are the CEO of a, uh, board game company. You are hilarious and amazing.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:20.720
I mean, I wouldn't-- I don't know that I'm the CEO.
I'm just helpin' make it work.

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:24.240
But DFTBA games has a new,
fun, weird, silly party game! It's called ROLF.

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.320
You can watch a video of me playing it
or check it out on Kickstarter.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:29.920
We'll be kickstarting for the next 24 days or so.

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:32.220
We've already reached our goal,
so we are making the game.

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:35.300
And John, I know that you can't come
to VidCon Australia, but I will be there.

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:38.680
If you're near enough to Melbourne or in Melbourne,
I have a coupon code for you.

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.020
Just use coupon code "Hank".

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:42.388
Looking forward to seeing all the people
who can come to VidCon Australia.

00:03:42.389 --> 00:03:44.220
John, I will see you on Tuesday.

